---
// Figure 4: atomic slot migration (Redis 8.4 and later), step by step.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import './cluster.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="cluster-migration" title="Atomic slot migration, step by step" controls="steps"
  caption="A sequence of steps, not a timed simulation. The order of events follows Redis's CLUSTER MIGRATION reference and its source (src/cluster_asm.c, 8.10.3); on my laptop the whole move of 4,096 slots took about 3 seconds. Left out: the memory the fork costs node C, and the many slots a real reshard moves together.">
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>Node C owns slot 12456 and node A is to take it. Node A asks for the slot; node C forks, takes a snapshot of the slot's keys and streams it to node A, then streams every later write to the slot. Throughout, node C still owns the slot and holds every key, so the client's reads are ordinary hits and its writes succeed, with no ASK or TRYAGAIN. When node A is within 1 MB of node C, node C pauses writes to the slot for a moment, node A takes ownership in one step, and node C answers MOVED from then on. The client follows one MOVED to node A and finds the key there. Node C then deletes its old copies, in the background unless a client is tracking keys, in which case it deletes them on its main thread so that it can send invalidations.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { sequenceFigure } from '../shared/sequence.mjs';
  import { asmSpec } from './sequences.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="cluster-migration"]').forEach(root => sequenceFigure(root, { ...asmSpec }));
</script>
